{"author":{"id":"8d2e0405f08ac6c481f912544d6d5c0f58b2dc14a7e51ad4d499ee7a34567943"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1795713936,"nonce":"51nWm+im8GPlOtZwKQ2lCqJfBQU=","message":"Measured: the default pseudo-inverse cut makes the ENGD result depend on the CPU thread count\n\nThe suspected cause in the description (run-to-run differences in CPU reductions feeding `lstsq` with the default cut on an ill-conditioned Gram matrix) is confirmed, and it is deterministic per thread count rather than random.\n\nSetup: the plain `engd(line_search=True)` optimizer of this test (Poisson2D, seed 0, 200 epochs, CPU, float64), run in separate processes pinned to a different number of cores. Final rel-L2:\n\n    cores   default rcond (eps scale)   rcond=1e-8\n    1       4.6e-8                      2.928e-4\n    2       5.2e-8                      2.928e-4\n    3       2.6e-7                      2.928e-4\n    4       7.8e-7                      2.928e-4\n    6       5.5e-7                      2.928e-4\n    8       1.3e-7                      2.928e-4\n    12      5.5e-7                      2.928e-4\n\nWith the default cut, the result moves by a factor of 17 with the thread count: eigenvalues of the Gram matrix near eps times its largest are kept or dropped depending on the summation order. With `rcond=1e-8` every run agrees to the digits printed, but that cut floors the error at 2.9e-4, so it is not a free fix. The test's 1e-3 threshold is passed in all of these runs; the occasional 2e-3 reported here was not reproduced.\n\nConsequences: a convergence test on this optimizer is machine-dependent, and so is any user result near the cut. The default is a separate decision from the test: a larger default cut costs accuracy (as above), while the eps-scale default makes results non-reproducible across machines. Closing this by a change to the test alone leaves the default as it is. I suggest a separate issue or a documented warning on `rcond`.","files":null}]}